WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.964
Dynamic query behavior is common.

2
00:00:01.964 --> 00:00:09.007
Users may choose optional filters, search fields, sort fields, sort
direction, page size, date ranges, or report columns.

3
00:00:09.007 --> 00:00:11.378
Administrators may build saved reports.

4
00:00:11.378 --> 00:00:14.222
Internal tools may expose broad search features.

5
00:00:14.222 --> 00:00:19.166
These are legitimate requirements, but they should be constrained by
explicit rules.

6
00:00:19.166 --> 00:00:27.090
The application should not allow request data to choose arbitrary model
attributes, SQL fragments, relationship paths, or expressions.

7
00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:31.966
A safe pattern maps user choices to approved internal field names or query
functions.

8
00:00:31.966 --> 00:00:37.994
For example, a public API might accept a small set of sort options and map
each option to a known model field.

9
00:00:37.994 --> 00:00:42.938
A report builder might expose predefined filters rather than free-form
query syntax.

10
00:00:42.938 --> 00:00:48.491
A search feature might select from known searchable fields and apply
consistent normalization.

11
00:00:48.491 --> 00:00:54.519
This keeps the user-facing feature flexible while keeping the query
structure under developer control.

12
00:00:54.519 --> 00:00:57.702
Pagination and limits are part of dynamic query safety.

13
00:00:57.702 --> 00:01:01.698
Unbounded result sets can create availability and privacy problems.

14
00:01:01.698 --> 00:01:11.247
A list endpoint that allows broad filters, nested relationship expansion,
or arbitrary page sizes can expose more data than intended even if no
injection flaw exists.

15
00:01:11.247 --> 00:01:19.442
Safe dynamic queries combine constrained structure, authorization-aware
filtering, reasonable limits, and predictable response shapes.

16
00:01:19.442 --> 00:01:23.370
Administrative queries deserve the same discipline as public APIs.

17
00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:30.075
Admin tools often run with more privilege and expose more fields, so
unsafe dynamic behavior can have a larger impact.

18
00:01:30.075 --> 00:01:39.760
Teams should review admin filters, exports, dashboard builders, and
support search tools for direct user control over attributes,
relationships, and raw conditions.

19
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:46.600
Dynamic behavior should be powerful because it is well designed, not
because it accepts whatever string a caller sends.
